# Listen-Fire self-host — copy to `deploy/.env` and fill in.
#
# This file is YOURS: the things only you know. Everything machine-generated
# (database password, token secret, both encryption keys, the team id, the API
# key) is minted on first boot into the `listen-fire-config` docker volume and must
# never be edited or regenerated. Back that volume up.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ model ---
# Any ONE of these is what gives this installation agents, extraction and
# arbitration of conflicting facts. Anthropic is the default provider for the
# agents. Without one the stack starts and serves — the graph, the CRM writes,
# the query surfaces and the whole of core work — and those three fail at the
# moment they are asked for, naming the key. `init` and the API both warn at
# every boot. Leave it empty for a demo, where there is nothing to call.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# KNOWLEDGE_LLM_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# Off by default, trusting the model. `1` (or `true`) puts an extraction's
# reply on a tight, input-sized output leash instead of the normal flat
# ceiling — only worth setting for a provider/model that is known to loop.
# EXTRACTION_OUTPUT_BUDGET=

# --------------------------------------------------------------- version ---
# WHICH release this installation runs. `up.sh` and `docker compose` pull
# `ghcr.io/listen-fire/<name>:<this>`; unset means `latest`, which moves under
# you the next time anything pulls. PINNING A TAG HERE IS HOW AN INSTALLATION
# CHOOSES WHEN IT MOVES: it stays on that release until you edit this line and
# restart. See UPGRADING.md for what an upgrade involves.
# LISTEN_FIRE_VERSION=v0.1.0

# ------------------------------------------------------------- safeguards ---
# The most network calls one automation run may make to Affinity before it is
# stopped and fails, naming the limit. Default 2000 — high enough that a large
# but honest run never notices, low enough that a read which forgot to narrow
# itself stops before it eats the API quota.
# AFFINITY_MAX_CALLS_PER_RUN=2000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- naming ---
LISTEN_FIRE_TEAM_NAME=Acme
LISTEN_FIRE_ADMIN_EMAIL=you@example.com
# The name your Slack app shows in a workspace — you register that app, so the
# name belongs to you. Defaults to `Listen-Fire`.
# SLACK_APP_DISPLAY_NAME=
# The app name Meta substitutes into your WhatsApp authentication template —
# the template is registered per deployment. Defaults to `Listen-Fire`.
# WHATSAPP_APP_DISPLAY_NAME=
# Where a user with a problem is told to go. No default: unset means this
# installation publishes no address rather than inventing one.
# SUPPORT_EMAIL=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- ports ---
# HOST ports. The containers always listen on their own fixed ports. Port
# NUMBERS only — the address they bind to is LISTEN_FIRE_BIND, below.
#
# THIS FILE WINS, including under `up.sh`: it supplies these (and API_BASE_URL /
# WEB_BASE_URL below) only as defaults for an installation that names none.
WEB_PORT=8080
API_PORT=8081
ADMIN_PORT=8082
FAKE_CHANNELS_PORT_HOST=8083
# WHICH host address those ports answer on. `127.0.0.1` is what a deployment
# behind a reverse proxy on the same box wants: the proxy reaches the stack and
# nothing else can, so the firewall stops being the only thing between the
# internet and a plain-HTTP port. Unset means every interface, which is what a
# trial on your own machine wants.
# LISTEN_FIRE_BIND=127.0.0.1

# ----------------------------------------------------------- composition ---
# WHICH units run, and which identity provider validates credentials. `up.sh`
# derives both from the units you name on its command line and exports them, so
# leave them commented for a trial. Set them here when you run `docker compose`
# yourself — which is what a deployment behind a real hostname does. Unset means
# every unit, with core identity. The process checks the pair against each other
# at boot in both directions: `core` in the product list and `core` identity
# imply each other, and either half without the other refuses to start.
# LISTEN_FIRE_PRODUCTS=knowledge,automations
# LISTEN_FIRE_PRINCIPAL=static

# ------------------------------------------------------------ datastores ---
# WHICH optional services this composition runs. `up.sh` names these on the
# command line itself; this line is what a bare `docker compose` reads. Leave it
# alone: the three datastore profiles below turn themselves OFF when you name a
# datastore of your own, and `admin` (the operator console) and `demo` (the
# stand-in third parties) are the two you might add by hand.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=postgres,redis,minio
#
# Postgres, Redis and the object store are bundled, and each can be moved to a
# managed service on its own by naming it here — nothing else changes, and the
# bundled service stops being part of the composition. See SELF_HOSTING.md,
# "Bringing your own datastores", and your platform's guide for the runbook that
# moves the data.
#
# Your own database. Set DATABASE_URL_READONLY too only if you have a replica;
# left unset it follows DATABASE_URL, which is what it must be either way.
# Run deploy/postgres-init/00-roles.sql against it, as a superuser, FIRST.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host:5432/listenfire
# DATABASE_URL_READONLY=
# Your own Redis. Host and port, no URL form: the client is built from these two
# and nothing else, so a Redis that demands AUTH or TLS cannot be reached.
# MESSAGE_QUEUE_REDIS_HOSTNAME=
# MESSAGE_QUEUE_REDIS_PORT=6379
# Your own object store — see "file storage" below, which is the same switch.

# ------------------------------------------------------------- graph mode ---
# `webhook` delivers graph mutations to the subscriptions you register; `local`
# hands them to an in-process consumer that only exists when `automations` is
# mounted in this same process. `up.sh` sets this for you from the units you
# name — override only if you know why.
# KNOWLEDGE_MUTATION_DELIVERY=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ urls ---
# Where this installation is reachable from the internet. Unset, `up.sh`
# defaults both to localhost for a trial; set them here for anything behind a
# real hostname and they are used as written, `up.sh` included.
# They also decide cookie security: the session cookie is `Secure` only when
# these are https, and a `Secure` cookie on plain http is silently dropped.
# API_BASE_URL=
# WEB_BASE_URL=
# Where an OAuth consent screen returns the user. Those callback paths are
# pages in the WEB app, so this is the WEB origin — and compose defaults it to
# WEB_BASE_URL. Set it only if the web app answers somewhere else.
# OAUTH_REDIRECT_BASE_URL=
# The origin a third party fetches an exposed file's bytes from — the API's,
# which is what compose defaults it to. (The code's own fallback is
# OAUTH_REDIRECT_BASE_URL and then a localhost literal, so off compose set it.)
# EXPOSED_FILE_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# ------------------------------------------------------------ integrations ---
# Every one of these is optional. Absent means the corresponding connector is
# simply not offered; nothing else changes. See SELF_HOSTING.md, "Registering
# your own third-party apps", for what to register and what each one costs.
# SLACK_MOVEMENTS_CLIENT_ID=
# SLACK_MOVEMENTS_CLIENT_SECRET=
# SLACK_MOVEMENTS_SIGNING_SECRET=
# SLACK_MOVEMENTS_STATE_SECRET=
# SLACK_MOVEMENTS_REDIRECT_URI=
# Attio: without this pair, Attio connects with a pasted workspace access token.
# ATTIO_CLIENT_ID=
# ATTIO_CLIENT_SECRET=
# AIRTABLE_CLIENT_ID=
# AIRTABLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GOOGLE_INTEGRATIONS_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_INTEGRATIONS_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GMAIL_CLIENT_ID=
# GMAIL_CLIENT_SECRET=
# DROPBOX_CLIENT_ID=
# DROPBOX_CLIENT_SECRET=
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
# TELEGRAM_BOT_USERNAME=
# TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Email: pick ONE provider. Resend wins if both are set, inbound and out.
# MAILGUN_API_KEY=
# MAILGUN_SENDING_DOMAIN=
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# OUTBOUND_EMAIL_FROM=
# The address automations receive mail on — local part and domain. No default:
# one would tell your authors to forward their mail to somebody else's inbox.
# INBOUND_EMAIL_ADDRESS=inbox@example.com
# SENTRY_DSN=

# ------------------------------------------------------------- web search ---
# A Google Custom Search key and the search-engine id it belongs to. The
# plugins that go looking for something on the open web — the LinkedIn activity
# research and the website research — search through these; without both, those
# two plugins cannot run and every record they were pointed at comes back
# unenriched.
# GOOGLE_CUSTOM_SEARCH_API_KEY=
# GOOGLE_CX=

# ---------------------------------------------------------- file storage ---
# Features that handle a file need an S3-compatible bucket; there is no
# local-filesystem driver. Leave every line here unset and this installation's
# own MinIO is that bucket, on a docker volume, wired up for you.
#
# SETTING AWS_S3_ENDPOINT is what says the store is yours: the bundled MinIO
# then leaves the composition, and these four are yours to fill in — all four
# together, or no storage at all, and a missing one means file-touching steps
# fail naming what they wanted.
# AWS_S3_ENDPOINT=https://storage.googleapis.com
# AWS_DOCUMENT_S3_BUCKET=
# AWS_REGION=auto
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# MinIO and Ceph need path-style addressing. Only the literal lowercase `true`
# turns it on: `TRUE` and `1` read as false.
# AWS_S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=

# Telegram, WhatsApp, Twilio, the narrowing settings for an API key
# (LISTEN_FIRE_SCOPES, LISTEN_FIRE_ACCESS) and the rest are in SELF_HOSTING.md's
# environment reference, with what each one does when unset.
